---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('brevo')!;
const b = appBySlug('sendlane')!;
const path = '/compare/brevo-vs-sendlane';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/brevo-vs-sendlane');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/brevo-vs-sendlane');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Which account should I compare?",
    "answer": "Confirm the linked Brevo PushOwl account and the product Sendlane/Privy offers today before comparing migration terms."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I import without checking subscriptions?",
    "answer": "No. Brevo warns that importing Shopify data can overwrite contact attributes including subscription status. Reconcile permissions before releasing sends."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does the acquisition establish a new-account destination?",
    "answer": "No. Confirm the offered platform, contract and support path with Sendlane/Privy."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are these measured results?",
    "answer": "No. The acceptance exercises are proposed and the basket calculation is hypothetical."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Import authority and account continuity.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{label:'Evaluate',tool:a.name,reason:'Reconcile the linked Shopify account and consent before import.'},{label:'Evaluate',tool:b.name,reason:'Confirm the current product and continuity responsibilities.'}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Brevo</th><th>Sendlane</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Primary check</td><td>Reconcile the linked Shopify account and consent before import.</td><td>Confirm the current product and continuity responsibilities.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance</td><td>Prove identity, permission and waiting-send exits.</td><td>Prove entry, queue ownership and purchase exits.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Budget</td><td>Quote the actual account, channel usage and operating work.</td><td>Quote the offered product, features and migration work.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Establish both account destinations</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360014912879-Brevo-PushOwl-app-for-Shopify-Connect-your-Shopify-store-with-Brevo-PushOwl">Brevo’s Shopify guide</a> uses Brevo PushOwl and warns that data imports may overwrite existing contact attributes, including subscription status. Export suppressions before import. In a small reconciliation set, include an unsubscribed customer, email-only permission, SMS-only permission and no marketing permission. Check each result before any automation sends.</p>
<p><a href="https://docs.pushowl.com/en/articles/14033929-understanding-the-pushowl-and-brevo-relationship">PushOwl’s account guide</a> describes dashboard access and support for linking existing accounts. Confirm which account holds your Shopify audience when you already use Brevo directly. Its <a href="https://docs.pushowl.com/en/articles/14660088-source-of-truth-for-marketing-consent">consent authority guide</a> describes channel-specific Shopify override settings. Record the configured authority rather than assuming an old unsubscribe will survive every import or update.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">Privy confirms its acquisition of Sendlane</a>. The <a href="https://www.privy.com/sendlane-acquisition">acquisition page</a> presents a combined email, SMS and onsite offering, but does not establish standalone new-account terms. Ask which platform a new merchant receives, who contracts and bills, and which migration/support work is included. Existing Sendlane merchants should separately confirm login, sender, automation and data continuity; an acquisition alone proves neither immediate retirement nor unchanged onboarding. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Compare the import contract with the continuity contract</h2>
<p>Brevo is a candidate when your team can reconcile the Shopify import and own downstream queues. For Sendlane/Privy, require a demonstration in the actual offered account. Inventory embedded forms, popup offers, sending domains, historical orders, custom attributes, suppression lists and integrations. Specify what is transferred, rebuilt or left read-only, and name the person who approves each replacement. A successful CSV upload does not prove that embedded forms now send to the correct account.</p>
<h2>Inspect an event, not just a contact count</h2>
<p>Proposed acceptance exercise: submit a mobile form, start an identified checkout and complete a separate checkout while a reminder waits. Capture customer/store identifiers, permission source and time, checkout identifier, item variants, quantities, currency and return URL where available. Require the supplier to show the actual payload and usable fields; do not assume gift messages, financing-widget activity or a subscription toggle are exposed by a standard integration. Verify the embedded form’s offer and destination list independently of checkout entry.</p>
<h2>Keep recovery useful for the basket</h2>
<p>For gift or sizing friction, link to relevant assistance and verify the return URL keeps the intended variant. For an unavailable size, suppress the unavailable item or route to an approved alternative. For financing or a bundle-code issue, show the actual payment or offer terms before adding another discount. A B2B basket below MOQ needs a named human enquiry owner. Explain shipping charges and subscription terms only from fields or approved content you can verify; a repeat buyer should not receive a first-purchase promise.</p>
<h2>Own the queue and approval boundary</h2>
<p>Before cutover, assign one system to each waiting checkout and record the last send, next eligible action and expiry. Pause the old automation before releasing its replacement. Test duplicate events, purchase, opt-out and a changed cart while a message waits. Check suppression again at send time. A failed purchase exit is a release blocker; keep the new flow paused and restore the previous owner without replaying all imported records. Choose delays and contact caps from your store’s buying cycle and channel permissions, then review them with the merchant.</p>
<h2>Price the migration as work</h2>
<p>Request current quotes for account scope, audience, message usage, channel charges, support and migration. Add staff time for consent reconciliation, embedded forms, event mapping, template approval and queue cutover. For an existing Sendlane merchant, include continuity work even when the login stays the same. Compare that total with retaining the current setup; a low entry price is not the cost of an approved recovery workflow.</p>
<h2>Use an explicit fixed-cost basket model</h2>
<p>Hypothetical $323 basket at a 50% full-price gross margin: goods cost stays $161.50. Full-price gross profit is $161.50. An 8% discount removes $25.84 of revenue, leaving $297.16 revenue and $135.66 gross profit. This excludes shipping subsidies, payment fees, returns, messaging and platform costs. It is not a recovery result or a recommended incentive cap. Measure incremental orders with a suitably sized comparison group before treating attributed revenue as profit.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
